Falsifies `system_tests::user::futex_tests::a_waiter_whose_region_is_destroyed_leaves_the_futex_table`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect, in milestone 812 (`std::thread::spawn` runs real threads in one address space)'s futex: the teardown of a blocked resident (`finish_blocked_resident`) forgets a futex waiter,
so a thread parked in `AddressSpace::WAIT` whose region is destroyed stays linked on the futex
table while its TCB page goes back to the region. The next wake of any key in that bucket walks it.

Expected to fail: "a destroyed waiter's TCB is still linked on the futex table". The per-key count
cannot see it, because the torn-down thread no longer records a key, which is why the test counts
links (replayed on all three architectures, 2026-10-10 UTC).

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/region_reaping.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched/region_reaping.rs b/kernel/src/sched/region_reaping.rs
index 30185504c..3253ea9d4 100644
--- a/kernel/src/sched/region_reaping.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched/region_reaping.rs
@@ -98,12 +98,7 @@ pub(super) fn finish_blocked_resident(sched: &mut IpcTables, tid: ThreadId) {
             }
         }
         // On the futex table under its key, where a later `WAKE` would walk a freed TCB.
-        Some(Wait::Futex(key)) => {
-            let ptr = thread_control_block_ptr(sched, tid);
-            if let Some(token) = sched.futexes.remove(key, ptr) {
-                hold_token(token);
-            }
-        }
+        Some(Wait::Futex(_)) => {}
         // A suspended thread (§300 (the paused state, a TCB verb, silent)) is on no queue and
         // already holds its token.
         Some(Wait::Resume) | None => {}
